    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Since 1939 twelve Mw>6.7 earthquakes have ruptured over 1200 km of the North Anatolian fault (NAF) that spans northern Turkey. The Marmara Sea, with Istanbul on its northern shore, remains the last unruptured portion of the NAF since 1700 and is well recognized as an extremely hazardous seismic gap. The fault structure within the large pull-apart basin that defines the Marmara Sea has been interpreted from several seismic reflection data sets and different researchers have produced substantially different fault interpretations. Constraining the fault geometry within the Marmara Sea is the first step in characterizing the imminent seismic hazard in this region. In this paper I detail a project designed to place mechanical constraints on the proposed fault geometries using existing seismic data sets, earthquake data, and Poly3D numerical modeling.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
